// Grade 4 passages (validated by live-patches/grade-journey/validate.mjs). Built by validate.mjs --write.
export const PASSAGES = [{"id":"g4-u1-p01","unit":"G4-U1","pool":"practice","title":"The Pepper Garden","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every spring, Nadia and her grandmother planted a garden behind their apartment building. The garden was only a narrow strip of dirt between the parking lot and the fence, but Grandma called it “our little farm.”\n\nThis year Nadia wanted to grow the biggest peppers on the block. She planted six pepper seeds in a row and watered them every single morning before school. When the first green sprouts poked out of the soil, she ran upstairs to tell Grandma.\n\n“Good,” Grandma said. “Now comes the hard part. Now you wait.”\n\nNadia did not like waiting. Each afternoon she crouched beside the plants and stared at them, as if staring could make them grow faster. She gave them extra water. She even dug up one plant and moved it to a sunnier spot. That plant drooped and turned yellow, and two days later it was gone.\n\nNadia felt terrible. “I was only trying to help,” she told Grandma.\n\nGrandma put an arm around her. “Plants grow on their own clock,” she said. “Our job is to give them what they need, and then trust them.”\n\nAfter that, Nadia watered the peppers once a day and left them alone. She pulled weeds from the row on Saturdays. She wrote the height of each plant in a small notebook. Week by week, the numbers grew, even when it looked like nothing was happening.\n\nOne week in July, a hot spell dried the soil until it cracked. Nadia wanted to pour buckets of water on the plants, but she remembered what Grandma had said. She watered them deeply in the cool evening instead, and by morning the leaves had perked up again.\n\nBy August, the five remaining plants were as tall as her knees. Shiny green peppers hung from every stem. Some were turning red at the bottom, as if they were blushing.\n\nNadia picked the first ripe pepper and carried it upstairs as carefully as an egg. Grandma sliced it into a pan with onions and rice. It was the best dinner Nadia could remember.\n\n“Next year,” Nadia said, “I'm going to plant tomatoes too.”\n\nGrandma laughed. “Then you had better start practicing your patience right now.”","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"What is the theme of the story?","options":["Growing things takes patience and care.","Peppers always grow best in full sun.","Grandparents are the best cooks of all."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"Which sentence is the best summary of the story?","options":["Nadia learns to wait, and her pepper plants grow and ripen.","Nadia moves a plant, and Grandma cooks onions and rice.","Nadia and Grandma decide to grow tomatoes on a farm."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Nadia's hurry caused a problem?","options":["Each afternoon she crouched beside the plants and stared at them, as if staring could make them grow faster.","That plant drooped and turned yellow, and two days later it was gone.","Shiny green peppers hung from every stem."],"answer":1,"evidence":"That plant drooped and turned yellow, and two days later it was gone."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the story, what does the word drooped mean?","options":["hung down weakly","grew very fast","turned bright red"],"answer":0,"evidence":"That plant drooped and turned yellow, and two days later it was gone."}],"grade":4,"words":365,"auto":{"fk":4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u1-p02","unit":"G4-U1","pool":"practice","title":"The Relay Team","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Marcus was the fastest runner in the fourth grade, and everyone knew it. So when Coach Rivera announced a class relay race for Field Day, Marcus expected to be the captain.\n\nInstead, Coach Rivera picked names out of a hat. Marcus ended up on a team with Priya, who had sprained her ankle last month, Ben, who usually walked during laps, and Sofia, who had just moved from Chile and hardly spoke in class.\n\n“We're going to lose,” Marcus muttered.\n\nAt their first practice, Marcus ran his part of the race so fast that he reached Ben before Ben was ready. The baton hit the grass. Marcus groaned loudly, and Ben's face turned pink.\n\nThat night, Marcus thought about the dropped baton. Being fast did not help if the handoff failed. A relay was four people, not one.\n\nThe next day, he tried something different. He asked each teammate what they were good at. Priya said she was great at starting quickly. Ben admitted he was slow, but he never dropped things. Sofia smiled shyly and said, in careful English, that she could run well around curves.\n\nMarcus thought about it. “Then Priya should go first,” he said. “Sofia can take the curve. Ben, you take the straight part, and I'll finish.”\n\nAll week, they practiced handoffs instead of sprints. Ben showed them how to hold out a hand without looking back. Sofia counted steps out loud in Spanish, and soon everyone was counting with her.\n\nOn Thursday, Priya's ankle felt sore, so she sat out and cheered from the bench. Marcus ran an extra turn in her place without complaining. Nobody dropped the baton once that week, and Ben began to grin every time it slapped into his palm.\n\nOn Field Day, their team did not have the fastest runners. But every handoff was smooth. Priya burst off the line, Sofia swept around the curve, Ben held steady, and Marcus crossed the finish line just ahead of the next team.\n\nThe four of them collapsed into a laughing pile on the grass.\n\n“We won,” Ben said, amazed.\n\n“We won together,” Marcus said, and he meant it.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which sentence best states the theme of the story?","options":["A team does best when it uses each person's strengths.","The fastest runner should always be the team captain.","Field Day races are won by practicing sprints daily."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Why did Ben's face most likely turn pink at the first practice?","options":["He felt embarrassed about the dropped baton.","He was tired from running a very long way.","He was excited to be picked for the team."],"answer":0},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Marcus do the day after he thought about the dropped baton?","options":["He asked each teammate what they were good at.","He practiced sprints by himself before school.","He asked the coach to put him on a new team."],"answer":0,"evidence":"He asked each teammate what they were good at."},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the story?","options":["Marcus learns to work with his team, and they win the relay together.","Marcus drops a baton, so Coach Rivera picks a brand new captain.","Sofia teaches the class Spanish, and Ben learns to run faster."],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":357,"auto":{"fk":4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u1-c01","unit":"G4-U1","pool":"check","title":"The Bowl That Kept Falling","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Mateo had watched the pottery teacher, Ms. Okafor, make a bowl in less than five minutes. She set a lump of wet clay on the spinning wheel, pressed her thumbs into the middle, and pulled the walls up as smooth as a seashell. It looked easy, but Mateo was about to discover that it was not easy at all.\n\nOn his first try, Mateo pressed much too hard on the slippery clay. The clay wobbled, leaned, and then flopped over like a tired puppy. On his second try, he spun the wheel too fast, and a chunk of clay flew off and smacked the wall. The kids at the next table giggled.\n\nMateo's cheeks burned. He squeezed the clay into a ball and dropped it into the bucket of scraps.\n\n“I'm just not good at this,” he muttered.\n\nMs. Okafor pulled up a stool beside him. She did not say that his bowl had been fine, because it hadn't. Instead, she pointed to a shelf in the back of the room. It was crowded with lopsided cups, cracked plates, and bowls with walls as thin as paper.\n\n“Those are mine from my first year,” she said. “I kept every one.”\n\nMateo stared at the crooked collection in disbelief. “Why would you ever keep the bad ones?”\n\n“So I remember that every good bowl starts as a bad one,” she said. “Your hands are learning. They just learn slowly.”\n\nShe showed him how to wet his hands so the clay would not drag. She showed him how to tuck his elbows against his knees so his arms stayed steady. Then she wandered off to help another student and left him alone to practice.\n\nMateo tried again, concentrating carefully, but the bowl collapsed anyway. He patiently tried a fourth time. This time the walls rose an inch before they sagged. On the fifth try, they rose two inches.\n\nBy the end of class, Mateo had made something that was almost a bowl. One side was taller than the other, and the rim had a dent where his thumb had slipped. But it stood up by itself.\n\nA week later, the bowl came out of the kiln, hard and pale. Mateo painted it a deep green and carried it home with both hands.\n\nHis little sister examined it and wrinkled her nose. “It's completely crooked.”\n\n“I know,” Mateo answered calmly. He set it on his windowsill, right where he would see it every morning. “That's how I'll remember it was my first one.”","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"What is the theme of the story?","options":["Learning a new skill takes patience and practice.","Clay bowls should always be painted dark green.","Teachers should never keep their old work."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"Which sentence is the best summary of the story?","options":["Mateo breaks his teacher's bowl and must make her a new one.","Mateo struggles at pottery, gets help, and keeps trying until it works.","Mateo wins a pottery contest with a perfect green bowl at school."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Ms. Okafor once struggled too?","options":["She showed him how to wet his hands so the clay would not drag.","Mateo's cheeks burned.","“Those are mine from my first year,” she said."],"answer":2,"evidence":"“Those are mine from my first year,” she said."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Mateo put the bowl on his windowsill?","options":["He is proud of how far he has come.","He wants the paint to dry in the sun.","He plans to give it to his sister later."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"The clay “flopped over like a tired puppy.” What does this comparison show?","options":["The clay sagged and fell limply.","The clay made a loud barking sound.","The clay was warm and soft to touch."],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":420,"auto":{"fk":3.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u1-c02","unit":"G4-U1","pool":"check","title":"The Man Next Door","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"When Kenji's family moved to Maple Court, his mother warned him about only one thing. “The man next door likes his quiet,” she said. “Try not to bother him.”\n\nThe man next door was named Walter. He was tall and gray-haired, and he hardly ever smiled. When Kenji waved at him from the driveway, Walter just nodded and went back inside. When Kenji's soccer ball bounced into Walter's yard, Walter set it on the sidewalk without a word.\n\n“He doesn't like kids,” Kenji decided. He started walking the long way around the block so he would not have to pass Walter's porch.\n\nOne afternoon in October, Kenji heard a loud crash. He peeked over the fence. Walter was standing beside a toppled ladder, and dozens of tiny wooden pieces were scattered across his lawn.\n\nKenji hesitated. Then he climbed over the low part of the fence and began picking up the pieces. Some were shaped like little roofs. Others had round holes in the middle.\n\nWalter turned and saw him. For a moment, Kenji was sure he was in trouble. Then Walter pointed to his ear.\n\n“You'll have to speak up,” Walter said. “My hearing isn't what it used to be.”\n\nKenji suddenly understood. Walter had never heard him say hello. He had only seen a boy waving and running away.\n\n“What are these?” Kenji asked, loudly this time.\n\n“Birdhouses,” Walter said, and for the first time, he smiled. “I build them for the school down the street. I was trying to hang the first one on that tree.”\n\nKenji held the ladder steady while Walter climbed. Together they hung a red birdhouse on the highest branch. Then Walter showed him the workshop in his garage, where a dozen more birdhouses waited for paint.\n\nAll that fall, Kenji spent Saturday mornings in Walter's garage. He learned to sand wood smooth and to hammer a nail without bending it. Walter learned that Kenji liked bright colors and loud jokes.\n\nIn the spring, when the first wren moved into the red birdhouse, Kenji ran next door to tell Walter. This time, he did not walk the long way around.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which sentence best states the theme of the story?","options":["Get to know someone before you judge them.","Always listen to warnings from your parents.","Building birdhouses is a good fall hobby."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the story?","options":["Kenji thinks his neighbor is unfriendly, then learns he cannot hear well and they become friends.","Kenji knocks over a ladder next door, so he must help his neighbor paint birdhouses.","Walter teaches the school to build birdhouses, and a wren moves into the red one."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Kenji had misunderstood Walter?","options":["Some were shaped like little roofs.","Walter had never heard him say hello.","He started walking the long way around the block so he would not have to pass Walter's porch."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Walter had never heard him say hello."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the author end the story with Kenji skipping the long way around?","options":["To show that Kenji no longer avoided Walter.","To show that Kenji was in a big hurry.","To show that the short way was safer now."],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the story, what does the word toppled mean?","options":["fallen over","painted red","very tall"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Walter was standing beside a toppled ladder, and dozens of tiny wooden pieces were scattered across his lawn."}],"grade":4,"words":358,"auto":{"fk":3.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u1-c03","unit":"G4-U1","pool":"check","title":"Rocks in the Park","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Zoe found the first rock by accident. It was a smooth gray stone at the edge of the creek, about the size of her palm. On a whim, she took it home and painted a yellow sun on it. Underneath, in tiny letters, she wrote: You are brighter than you think.\n\nThe next morning, on her way to school, she left the rock on a bench in Lincoln Park.\n\nAfter that, Zoe couldn't stop. She painted a turtle that said Slow days are okay. She painted a rainbow that said Someone is glad you are here. Every few days, she left a new rock somewhere in the park: beside the swings, under the big pine, next to the water fountain.\n\nAt first, it was exciting. But after two weeks, Zoe began to wonder if anyone even noticed. The rocks disappeared, but nobody ever mentioned them. Maybe people just threw them away.\n\nThen came a terrible Thursday. Zoe forgot her lines in the class play. Her best friend sat with someone else at lunch. On the walk home, it started to rain, and her shoes got soaked.\n\nZoe stomped through the park with her head down. She almost didn't see the rock on the bench where she had left her very first one.\n\nIt wasn't one of hers. It was painted bright purple, with a crooked flower in the middle. Someone had written on it in shaky marker: Thank you for the rocks. They made my week.\n\nZoe picked it up. Under the bench, she spotted another rock, and then another. One said Keep going. One had a smiling cloud. One, painted by a much smaller hand, just said HI in giant letters.\n\nZoe stood in the rain, holding the stones, and laughed out loud. People had noticed after all. They had not thrown her rocks away. They had been painting rocks of their own.\n\nThat night, Zoe set the purple rock on her windowsill where she could see it every morning. Then she got out her paints. She had a lot of new rocks to make, and now she knew exactly who they were for: anyone who needed one.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"What is the theme of the story?","options":["Small acts of kindness can spread to others.","It is best to stay inside on rainy days.","Painting is harder than it first looks."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"Which sentence is the best summary of the story?","options":["Zoe leaves kind painted rocks in a park and learns others are doing it too.","Zoe forgets her lines in a play, so she decides to give up painting rocks.","Zoe finds a purple rock at the creek and paints a yellow sun on it."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Zoe's rocks had inspired other people?","options":["They had been painting rocks of their own.","The rocks disappeared, but nobody ever mentioned them.","Zoe stomped through the park with her head down."],"answer":0,"evidence":"They had been painting rocks of their own."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the author describe Zoe's terrible Thursday?","options":["To show that Zoe needed kindness herself when she found the rocks.","To explain why Zoe stopped walking through Lincoln Park.","To show that the class play was the main problem in the story."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What was written on the purple rock?","options":["Thank you for the rocks.","You are brighter than you think.","Slow days are okay."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Someone had written on it in shaky marker: Thank you for the rocks."}],"grade":4,"words":359,"auto":{"fk":2.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u2-p01","unit":"G4-U2","pool":"practice","title":"Seeing with Sound","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"Diagram: a bat's call travels to a moth and bounces back as an echo","text":"On a summer evening, you might see small dark shapes darting over a pond. They swoop, twist, and turn sharply in the fading light. These fliers are not birds. They are bats, the only mammals that can truly fly. Many bats hunt insects at night, when it is too dark to see well. So how do they find a tiny moth in the dark? They use their ears.\n\nPeople sometimes say that someone is “blind as a bat,” but that saying is wrong. Bats can see. However, many bats depend more on a skill called echolocation. The word comes from echo, a sound that bounces back.\n\nHere is how it works. First, a bat sends out a burst of high squeaks through its mouth or nose. Most of these sounds are so high that human ears cannot hear them. Next, the sound waves travel through the air until they hit something, such as a tree branch or a beetle. Then the waves bounce back to the bat as echoes. The bat's large ears catch the echoes, and its brain turns them into a kind of sound picture.\n\nThat sound picture holds a lot of information. If an echo returns quickly, the object is close. If it takes longer, the object is farther away. The echoes can even tell a bat how big an insect is and which way it is moving. Some bats can sense a wire as thin as a human hair.\n\nAs a bat closes in on its dinner, it squeaks faster and faster. Scientists call this quick burst of calls a feeding buzz. Right at the end, the bat snatches the insect, often scooping it up with a wing or its tail.\n\nNot every bat uses echolocation. Many large fruit bats find food mostly by sight and smell. They do not need to chase flying insects, so they can rely on their big eyes and sharp noses instead.\n\nBats are not the only animals that see with sound. Dolphins and some whales use clicks to find fish in dark or cloudy water. People borrowed the same idea to build sonar, which helps ships measure how deep the water is and find objects underwater.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Many bats find their way and their food by listening to echoes.","Bats are blind, so they must stay close to the ground at night.","Fruit bats are the largest and fastest flying mammals on Earth."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that echoes tell a bat how far away things are?","options":["Bats can see.","If an echo returns quickly, the object is close.","Right at the end, the bat snatches the insect, often scooping it up with a wing or its tail."],"answer":1,"evidence":"If an echo returns quickly, the object is close."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happens right after the bat's sound waves hit an object?","options":["The bat sends out a burst of high squeaks.","The waves bounce back to the bat as echoes.","The bat's brain draws a map of the pond."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Then the waves bounce back to the bat as echoes."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the passage, what is a feeding buzz?","options":["a fast burst of calls near prey","a loud hum made by moth wings","a sound bats make while resting"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Scientists call this quick burst of calls a feeding buzz."}],"grade":4,"words":366,"auto":{"fk":3.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u2-p02","unit":"G4-U2","pool":"practice","title":"The Secret of Spider Silk","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Early on a fall morning, you may spot a round web shining with dew between two branches. It looks delicate, as if a breath of wind could tear it apart. Yet that web is made of one of the toughest materials in nature: spider silk.\n\nA spider makes silk inside its body. Special glands in its belly hold the silk as a thick liquid. At the tip of the belly are tiny parts called spinnerets. As the spider pulls the liquid out through its spinnerets, it turns into a solid thread. For its weight, spider silk is stronger than steel. It can also stretch a long way before it snaps.\n\nMany spiders can make several different kinds of silk. Some silk is dry and strong. Some is sticky. Some is soft and fluffy, and spiders use it to wrap their eggs.\n\nA garden spider uses these silks to build a round web called an orb. First, it lets out a thread and waits for the wind to carry it to a nearby branch. When the thread catches, the spider has a bridge. Next, it adds a frame and a set of lines that run from the center like the spokes of a wagon wheel. Finally, it spins a sticky thread around and around in a spiral. The whole job can take less than an hour.\n\nWhy doesn't the spider get stuck in its own trap? It walks mostly on the dry spokes and avoids the sticky spiral. Its legs also have tiny hairs and a special coating that help it move without getting glued down.\n\nWhen an insect flies into the web, the sticky silk holds it tight. The web shakes, and the spider feels the shaking through its legs. It rushes over and wraps its meal in silk.\n\nSpiders waste very little. Many garden spiders eat their old web and use the silk to make a new one.\n\nNot all spiders build webs, though. Jumping spiders and wolf spiders chase their food instead. Even so, they still trail a safety line of silk behind them, ready to catch them if they fall.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Spiders make strong silk and use it in many clever ways.","Spider webs are weak, so spiders must rebuild them every hour.","Garden spiders are the only spiders that can make any silk."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that spider silk is very tough?","options":["Some is soft and fluffy, and spiders use it to wrap their eggs.","For its weight, spider silk is stronger than steel.","The whole job can take less than an hour."],"answer":1,"evidence":"For its weight, spider silk is stronger than steel."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What does a garden spider do after it makes a bridge?","options":["It spins a sticky spiral right away.","It eats its old web to save silk.","It adds a frame and lines like spokes."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Next, it adds a frame and a set of lines that run from the center like the spokes of a wagon wheel."},{"type":"why","q":"How does a spider keep from sticking to its own web?","options":["It walks on dry spokes and has coated legs.","It waits for the dew to wash the glue off.","It only walks across the web at night."],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":353,"auto":{"fk":3.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u2-c01","unit":"G4-U2","pool":"check","title":"Maria Mitchell's Comet","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"On the night of October 1, 1847, a young woman climbed onto the roof of a bank building on Nantucket, an island off the coast of Massachusetts. She carried a small telescope. Her name was Maria Mitchell, and that night she would make history.\n\nMaria was born on Nantucket in 1818. Many families on the island earned their living from the sea, and sailors needed to understand the stars to find their way across the ocean. Maria's father checked the instruments that ship captains used for this job. He also loved astronomy, the study of stars and planets. He believed that girls should get the same education as boys, which was an unusual idea at the time. By the age of twelve, Maria was helping him measure the exact time of an eclipse.\n\nAs an adult, Maria worked as a librarian. During the day, she read every science book she could find. At night, she studied the sky. She spent hours sweeping her telescope back and forth, slowly and carefully, looking for anything new.\n\nOn that October night, she spotted a fuzzy patch of light that had not been there before. She knew the sky so well that she was sure it did not belong. It was a comet, and no one had ever recorded it.\n\nNews of her discovery spread around the world. The king of Denmark had promised a gold medal to the first person to discover a comet with a telescope, and he awarded it to Maria. People began calling the comet “Miss Mitchell's Comet.” The next year, she became the first woman elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences.\n\nLater, Maria became a professor of astronomy at Vassar College, a new college for women. She was the first person hired to teach there. She took her students outside at night to observe the sky, and she told them to question everything. Many of her students went on to become scientists themselves.\n\nMaria Mitchell proved that great discoveries come from patience and careful watching. She also opened the door for many women who wanted to study science.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Maria Mitchell discovered a comet and helped women enter science.","Maria Mitchell worked as a librarian on the island of Nantucket.","Maria Mitchell's father checked instruments for ship captains."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Maria's father helped her become a scientist?","options":["Many of her students went on to become scientists themselves.","By the age of twelve, Maria was helping him measure the exact time of an eclipse.","She spent hours sweeping her telescope back and forth, slowly and carefully, looking for anything new."],"answer":1,"evidence":"By the age of twelve, Maria was helping him measure the exact time of an eclipse."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows how Maria knew the comet was new?","options":["She knew the sky so well that she was sure it did not belong.","She carried a small telescope.","During the day, she read every science book she could find."],"answer":0,"evidence":"She knew the sky so well that she was sure it did not belong."},{"type":"literal","q":"What did the king of Denmark give Maria?","options":["a gold medal","a new telescope","a job at a college"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The king of Denmark had promised a gold medal to the first person to discover a comet with a telescope, and he awarded it to Maria."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the author mention that her father's view of girls was unusual?","options":["To show that Maria had a rare chance most girls then did not have","To show that the people of Nantucket did not like astronomy","To explain why Maria later chose to work as a librarian"],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":352,"auto":{"fk":6.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u2-c02","unit":"G4-U2","pool":"check","title":"The Clever Octopus","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","caption":"Fact box: An octopus has eight arms and three hearts.","text":"If you could design a creature from scratch, you might not come up with anything as strange as the octopus. It has eight arms, three hearts, and no bones at all. Yet the octopus is one of the smartest animals in the sea. Scientists who study it are still discovering new surprises every year.\n\nAn octopus's body is soft, so it can squeeze into tight spaces. A large octopus can slip through a hole only a little bigger than its eye. This helps it hide from sharks, seals, and other hungry animals. It also helps the octopus sneak into cracks in the rocks to find crabs and clams to eat.\n\nThe octopus is also a master of disguise. Its skin is filled with thousands of special cells that hold color. By squeezing and relaxing these cells, an octopus can change its color in less than a second. Some octopuses can even change the bumps on their skin so they look like rough rock or waving seaweed. A hungry fish may swim right past without noticing anything at all.\n\nScientists have tested octopus smarts in many ways. In aquariums, octopuses have learned to unscrew the lids of jars to reach a crab inside. Some have figured out mazes. Others have learned to recognize the people who care for them, and they have squirted water at keepers they did not like.\n\nOne reason octopuses are so clever may be the way their bodies are built. An octopus has a large brain for its size, but most of its nerve cells are not in its head. About two out of every three are in its arms. Each arm can touch, taste, and grab things on its own. In a way, every arm is a little bit like a separate thinker.\n\nOctopuses face one big challenge, though. Most kinds live only one or two years. That means they have very little time to learn. Scientists are still amazed at how much an octopus can figure out in such a short life.\n\nThe next time you visit an aquarium, look closely at the octopus tank. The octopus may be looking right back at you.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["The octopus has amazing bodies and minds that help it survive.","The octopus lives only one or two years in the wild ocean.","The octopus has blue blood, which makes it hard to study."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that octopuses can solve problems?","options":["In aquariums, octopuses have learned to unscrew the lids of jars to reach a crab inside.","A large octopus can slip through a hole only a little bigger than its eye.","Some octopuses can even change the bumps on their skin so they look like rough rock or waving seaweed."],"answer":0,"evidence":"In aquariums, octopuses have learned to unscrew the lids of jars to reach a crab inside."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best explains how an octopus changes color?","options":["In aquariums, octopuses have learned to unscrew the lids of jars to reach a crab inside.","By squeezing and relaxing these cells, an octopus can change its color in less than a second.","This helps it hide from sharks, seals, and other hungry animals."],"answer":1,"evidence":"By squeezing and relaxing these cells, an octopus can change its color in less than a second."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where are most of an octopus's nerve cells?","options":["in its arms","in its head","in its hearts"],"answer":0,"evidence":"About two out of every three are in its arms."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author say every arm is like a separate thinker?","options":["To help readers picture how the arms act on their own","To prove that an octopus has eight small brains in its head","To show that octopus arms do not work together at all"],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":359,"auto":{"fk":5.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u2-c03","unit":"G4-U2","pool":"check","title":"The Science Fair Setback","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"For two weeks, Rosa Delgado had been building a water filter for the science fair. She cut a plastic bottle in half and turned the top part upside down like a funnel. Then she filled it with layers: cotton on the bottom, then sand, then tiny pebbles, then bigger gravel on top. Her plan was simple. She would pour muddy water through the filter, and clear water would drip out the bottom.\n\nThe night before the fair, Rosa tested it one last time. She poured in a cup of brown, cloudy water and waited. The water that dripped out was still the color of weak tea.\n\nRosa felt her face get hot. She had already printed her poster. It said, in big letters, MY FILTER MAKES WATER CLEAN.\n\nHer older brother, Mateo, leaned in the doorway. “So change the poster,” he said.\n\n“I can't change it,” Rosa snapped. “The fair is tomorrow.”\n\nMateo shrugged. “Scientists change their ideas all the time. That's sort of the whole point.”\n\nRosa sat on the floor and thought. Then she grabbed her notebook. If the filter didn't work, she could at least figure out why. She took the filter apart and tried again with the layers in a different order. The water came out a little lighter. She added a second layer of sand. It came out lighter still. She wrote down every result in a neat chart.\n\nIt was almost eleven o'clock when she finally stopped. The water still was not perfectly clear, but it was much better than before.\n\nAt the fair the next day, Rosa's poster had a new title: WHAT MAKES A FILTER WORK BETTER? Under it, she taped her chart and three jars of water, from darkest to lightest.\n\nA judge with a clipboard studied the jars for a long time. “Your first try didn't work,” she said. “Why did you keep going?”\n\nRosa took a breath. “Because I wanted to know what was wrong,” she said. “Every test told me something.”\n\nThe judge smiled and wrote a note. Rosa didn't win first place, but she won a ribbon for Best Use of the Scientific Method. She hung it over her desk, right next to the old poster she had never used.","questions":[{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Rosa was upset when her filter failed?","options":["Rosa felt her face get hot.","Then she grabbed her notebook.","She hung it over her desk, right next to the old poster she had never used."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Rosa felt her face get hot."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Rosa worked carefully like a scientist?","options":["Her older brother, Mateo, leaned in the doorway.","She wrote down every result in a neat chart.","She had already printed her poster."],"answer":1,"evidence":"She wrote down every result in a neat chart."},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Mateo mean when he says changing ideas is the whole point?","options":["Science is about learning from results, even surprising ones.","Rosa should give up and choose a much easier project.","Posters are the most important part of any science fair."],"answer":0},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Rosa do first after she grabbed her notebook?","options":["She tried again with the layers in a different order.","She added a second layer of sand to the bottle.","She taped three jars of water onto her poster."],"answer":0,"evidence":"She took the filter apart and tried again with the layers in a different order."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Rosa keep a poster she had never used?","options":["To remember how much she learned from her failed test","Because the judge asked her to bring it home with her","Because she planned to use it at next year's fair"],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":373,"auto":{"fk":3.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u3-p01","unit":"G4-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Sea Turtles and the Light","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: A Sea Turtle's First Night\n\nSea turtles spend almost their whole lives in the ocean. But every few years, a female swims back to a sandy beach to lay her eggs. Many females return to the same area where they hatched long ago.\n\nThe mother crawls up the beach at night. She digs a deep hole with her back flippers and lays about one hundred eggs, each one the size of a ping-pong ball. Then she covers the nest with sand and returns to the sea. She never sees her babies.\n\nThe eggs stay warm under the sand for about two months. Then the tiny hatchlings break out of their shells and dig their way up together. They usually come out at night, when the sand is cool and fewer birds are hunting.\n\nNext, the hatchlings must find the ocean. They do this by looking for light. On a natural beach, the brightest place at night is the open sky over the water, where moonlight and starlight shine on the waves. The hatchlings scramble toward that glow as fast as their flippers can carry them.\n\n## Text 2: Lights Out on Palm Beach\n\nImani is ten years old, and every summer morning she walks the beach near her home in Florida with her aunt. They are volunteers who count sea turtle nests.\n\nOne morning last July, Imani found something worrying. Dozens of tiny tracks led away from a nest, but they did not go toward the water. Instead, they wandered up the beach toward the bright windows of a hotel. Her aunt explained that the hatchlings had mistaken the hotel lights for the glow over the ocean.\n\nImani and her aunt gently gathered the lost hatchlings in a bucket of damp sand and carried them to the water's edge. Then Imani decided to do more. She wrote a letter to the town council asking for a rule about beach lights during nesting season.\n\nThe town agreed. Now, from May through October, buildings near the beach must use low, shaded lights or close their curtains at night. This summer, Imani has found far fewer lost tracks.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"What does Text 2 show that Text 1 only hints at?","options":["What happens when bright lights confuse hatchlings","How deep a mother turtle digs her nest hole","How long the eggs stay warm under the sand"],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Using both texts, why did the hatchlings crawl toward the hotel?","options":["They follow the brightest light, and the hotel was brighter than the sea.","They were looking for their mother, who had gone back up the beach.","They needed cool sand, and the hotel lawn was cooler than the beach."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"When must buildings near the beach use low lights?","options":["from May through October","from June through August","every night of the year"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Now, from May through October, buildings near the beach must use low, shaded lights or close their curtains at night."},{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of both texts together?","options":["Hatchlings need dark beaches to find their way to the ocean.","Volunteers in Florida count sea turtle nests every summer.","Female sea turtles lay about one hundred eggs at a time."],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":357,"auto":{"fk":5.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u3-p02","unit":"G4-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Islands Made of Fire","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: How a Volcano Works\n\nDeep under your feet, the Earth is extremely hot. In some places, it is hot enough to melt rock. Melted rock underground is called magma.\n\nMagma is lighter than the solid rock around it, so it slowly rises, the way a bubble rises in a glass of water. It collects in a pool called a magma chamber. Over time, pressure builds up inside the chamber. When the pressure gets too strong, the magma pushes up through cracks and bursts out at the surface. This is an eruption.\n\nOnce magma reaches the surface, it has a new name: lava. Some lava is thick and sticky, and it traps gas until it explodes. Other lava is thin and runny, and it flows out in glowing rivers. Either way, the lava cools and hardens into new rock. After many eruptions, layer upon layer of rock piles up and forms a mountain.\n\nMost volcanoes are found where the giant plates of Earth's crust meet. But a few form over hot spots, where magma rises in the middle of a plate.\n\n## Text 2: The Youngest Land on Earth\n\nThe Hawaiian Islands sit in the middle of the Pacific Ocean, thousands of miles from any continent. Every one of them was built by volcanoes.\n\nThe islands formed over a hot spot. For millions of years, lava has poured out of the sea floor there. Each eruption added another layer of rock until a mountain finally rose above the waves. As the Pacific plate slowly moved, new islands formed over the hot spot, one after another, like beads on a string.\n\nToday, the Big Island of Hawaii sits over the hot spot. Its volcano Kilauea is one of the most active on Earth. Kilauea's lava is thin and runny. Visitors to Hawaii Volcanoes National Park can sometimes watch it glow at night from a safe distance.\n\nWhen Kilauea's lava flows all the way to the coast, it pours into the ocean with clouds of steam. There it cools and hardens. In this way, the Big Island is still growing. Some of its beaches are younger than the people standing on them.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"How does Text 2 connect to the hot spots described in Text 1?","options":["It gives a real example of islands built over a hot spot.","It explains that hot spots are found only where plates meet.","It shows that hot spots make thick lava that explodes."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Using both texts, what kind of eruptions does Kilauea most likely have?","options":["Glowing rivers of lava that flow out","Explosions caused by trapped gas","Sticky lava that stays near the top"],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"In Text 1, what is magma?","options":["melted rock under the ground","a crack in the Earth's crust","a plate that moves very slowly"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Melted rock underground is called magma."},{"type":"main","q":"What idea do both texts share?","options":["Lava cools into rock that can build new land.","Volcanoes only form in the Pacific Ocean.","People should never visit an active volcano."],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":360,"auto":{"fk":5.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u3-c01","unit":"G4-U3","pool":"check","title":"Two Wheels Through Time","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: The First Bicycles\n\nThe bicycle you ride today took about seventy years to invent. In 1817, a German inventor named Karl von Drais built a wooden machine with two wheels and a seat. It had no pedals. Riders sat on it and pushed against the ground with their feet, a bit like a scooter. People called it a running machine.\n\nIn the 1860s, inventors in France attached pedals to the front wheel. These bikes were faster, but their wooden wheels with iron rims rattled so much on bumpy roads that people nicknamed them boneshakers.\n\nNext came the high-wheeler. Its front wheel was huge, sometimes taller than a grown man, because a bigger wheel traveled farther with each turn of the pedals. But riders sat way up high, and a small bump could send them tumbling over the handlebars.\n\nFinally, in the 1880s, the safety bicycle appeared. Its two wheels were the same size, and a chain connected the pedals to the back wheel. Soon after, soft rubber tires filled with air made the ride smooth. The safety bicycle was easier and safer to ride, so millions of people bought one.\n\n## Text 2: A Country on Bikes\n\nIn the Netherlands, a small country in Europe, bicycles are everywhere. The country has more bikes than people. Children ride to school, parents ride to work, and grandparents ride to the market with baskets full of groceries.\n\nWhy are bikes so popular there? One reason is that the land is very flat, so pedaling is easy. Another reason is planning. Dutch cities have built thousands of miles of bike lanes that are separated from cars. Many streets even have their own traffic lights just for bicycles. Because the lanes are safe, many parents let their children ride to school on their own.\n\nRiding a bike has many benefits. It is good exercise, and it does not pollute the air the way cars do. Bikes also take up much less space than cars, so streets are less crowded.\n\nToday, cities around the world are studying the Dutch example. Many are adding bike lanes of their own.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"How is Text 1 different from Text 2?","options":["Text 1 tells the history of bikes, and Text 2 tells how bikes are used today.","Text 1 tells about bike lanes, and Text 2 tells about the first bike inventors.","Text 1 argues that bikes are dangerous, and Text 2 argues that cars are better."],"answer":0},{"type":"compare","q":"What idea about safety appears in both texts?","options":["People ride more when riding feels safe.","Bikes should always have huge front wheels.","Riders must stay off of any city streets."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence from Text 2 best supports the idea that planning made biking popular?","options":["Dutch cities have built thousands of miles of bike lanes that are separated from cars.","It is good exercise, and it does not pollute the air the way cars do.","Children ride to school, parents ride to work, and grandparents ride to the market with baskets full of groceries."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Dutch cities have built thousands of miles of bike lanes that are separated from cars."},{"type":"literal","q":"What did the safety bicycle use to connect the pedals to the back wheel?","options":["a chain","a rubber belt","an iron rod"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Its two wheels were the same size, and a chain connected the pedals to the back wheel."},{"type":"inference","q":"Using both texts, which bike would Dutch grandparents most likely ride to the market?","options":["a bike like the safety bicycle","a bike like the high-wheeler","a bike like the running machine"],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":354,"auto":{"fk":5.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u3-c02","unit":"G4-U3","pool":"check","title":"Saving the Monarchs","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: The Great Monarch Journey\n\nEvery fall, millions of orange and black monarch butterflies in the eastern United States and Canada begin an amazing trip. They fly south, some traveling as far as three thousand miles, to reach the mountains of central Mexico. No other insect is known to make such a long journey every year.\n\nIn Mexico, the monarchs gather in cool forests of tall fir trees. So many butterflies cling to the branches that the trees look orange. The cool air helps them rest and save energy all winter long. Thick clusters of butterflies also help keep one another warm on cold mountain nights.\n\nIn spring, the monarchs fly north again. Along the way, the females lay eggs, and a new generation of butterflies continues the trip. Summer monarchs live only a few weeks. But the generation born at the end of summer can live for about eight months, long enough to fly all the way to Mexico. Scientists are still studying how these butterflies find a place they have never seen.\n\nSadly, the number of monarchs making this trip has dropped a great deal in recent years.\n\n## Text 2: A Garden for Caterpillars\n\nMarisol's class in Ohio wanted to help monarch butterflies. Their teacher explained a key fact: monarch caterpillars can eat only one kind of plant, called milkweed. Mother monarchs lay their eggs only on milkweed leaves. Without milkweed, there can be no new monarchs.\n\nMilkweed once grew in many fields and along many roads. But as land has been cleared for farms, houses, and parking lots, much of it has been lost.\n\nThe class decided to plant a milkweed garden next to the playground. They also added purple and yellow flowers, because adult butterflies drink sweet nectar from many kinds of blooms.\n\nBy August, the garden was buzzing. Marisol spotted striped caterpillars munching on the milkweed leaves. A few weeks later, she found a green chrysalis hanging under a leaf like a tiny jewel.\n\nMilkweed has another secret. It contains a bitter poison that does not hurt the caterpillars. Instead, it makes them, and the butterflies they become, taste terrible to birds.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"How do the two texts work together?","options":["Text 1 explains the monarchs' journey, and Text 2 shows one way to help them.","Text 1 explains milkweed gardens, and Text 2 describes the trip to Mexico.","Text 1 shows a class project, and Text 2 explains how birds find butterflies."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Using both texts, what is one likely reason fewer monarchs are making the trip?","options":["Much of the milkweed they need has been lost.","The fir trees in Mexico have grown too cold.","Birds have learned to like the taste of them."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that milkweed protects monarchs?","options":["They also added purple and yellow flowers, because adult butterflies drink sweet nectar from many kinds of blooms.","Instead, it makes them, and the butterflies they become, taste terrible to birds.","So many butterflies cling to the branches that the trees look orange."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Instead, it makes them, and the butterflies they become, taste terrible to birds."},{"type":"literal","q":"Where do eastern monarchs spend the winter?","options":["in the mountains of central Mexico","in the fields and farms of Ohio","in the warm forests of Canada"],"answer":0,"evidence":"They fly south, some traveling as far as three thousand miles, to reach the mountains of central Mexico."},{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of both texts together?","options":["Monarchs make an amazing trip, and they need milkweed to survive.","Monarch butterflies can live for eight months in the summer.","Students in Ohio planted flowers next to their school playground."],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":358,"auto":{"fk":6.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-u3-c03","unit":"G4-U3","pool":"check","title":"The Power of Sleep","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: Why Your Brain Needs Sleep\n\nYou spend about one third of your life asleep. That might sound like wasted time, but your body and brain are very busy while you sleep.\n\nDuring the day, your brain takes in a huge amount of information: new spelling words, the rules of a game, the name of a new friend. At night, your brain sorts through all of it. It stores the important memories and clears away what it does not need. That is one reason a good night of sleep can help you remember what you learned.\n\nSleep also helps your body grow and repair itself. Your muscles recover after a day of running and playing. Your body even fights germs better when you get enough rest.\n\nDoctors say that children from six to twelve years old need nine to twelve hours of sleep each night. Without enough sleep, it is harder to pay attention, solve problems, and stay in a good mood. The glowing light from phones and tablets can make it harder to fall asleep, so experts suggest turning screens off an hour before bed.\n\n## Text 2: My Sleep Experiment\n\nBy Omar, age 10\n\nMy science teacher said kids my age need at least nine hours of sleep. I usually stayed up until ten thirty watching videos on my tablet, and I woke up at six thirty. That is only eight hours. I decided to test what would happen if I slept more.\n\nWeek 1: I kept my normal schedule. I yawned all through math class. On Friday, I got six out of ten words right on my spelling quiz.\n\nWeek 2: I went to bed at nine o'clock and left my tablet in the kitchen. The first night, I could not fall asleep for a long time. By Wednesday, I was falling asleep fast. I felt less grumpy in the mornings, and my little sister said I was nicer at breakfast. On Friday, I got nine out of ten words right.\n\nMy conclusion: more sleep helped me. Next, I want to test whether it helps me score more goals in soccer.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"How do Omar's results in Text 2 connect to Text 1?","options":["They match the claim that sleep helps you remember what you learned.","They show that screens do not change how fast people fall asleep.","They prove that children need more than twelve hours of sleep."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence from Text 1 best explains why Omar's spelling score went up?","options":["Your muscles recover after a day of running and playing.","That is one reason a good night of sleep can help you remember what you learned.","The glowing light from phones and tablets can make it harder to fall asleep, so experts suggest turning screens off an hour before bed."],"answer":1,"evidence":"That is one reason a good night of sleep can help you remember what you learned."},{"type":"inference","q":"Using both texts, why did Omar most likely leave his tablet in the kitchen?","options":["Screen light can make it harder to fall asleep.","His sister wanted to watch videos on it.","His teacher said tablets cause bad grades."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"How many words did Omar get right on the quiz in Week 2?","options":["nine out of ten","six out of ten","eight out of ten"],"answer":0,"evidence":"On Friday, I got nine out of ten words right."},{"type":"main","q":"What main idea do the two texts share?","options":["Getting enough sleep helps you think and feel better.","Kids should stay up late on weekends to finish homework.","Science experiments work best when done at school."],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":354,"auto":{"fk":4.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-book-ch1","unit":"G4-U4","pool":"book","book":"The Seed Library Mystery","chapter":1,"title":"Chapter 1: The Empty Drawer","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Most libraries lend books. The Hillside Library lent seeds too.\n\nIn the corner of the children's room stood an old wooden cabinet with forty tiny drawers. Each drawer had a handwritten label: Sunflower, Green Bean, Cherry Tomato, Pumpkin. Inside were paper envelopes full of seeds. Anyone in town could borrow a few packets in the spring. They planted the seeds, grew the plants, and saved some of the new seeds at the end of the summer. In the fall, they brought those seeds back so that someone else could plant them next year.\n\nMaya Okafor was ten years old, and she had volunteered at the seed library every Saturday since March. Her job was to fold new envelopes, check the drawers, and write down what people borrowed in a big green notebook. Miss Ana, the librarian, said Maya kept the neatest records she had ever seen.\n\nOn the first Saturday in May, Maya opened the Pumpkin drawer and frowned. It was empty again.\n\nShe flipped through the green notebook. Sunflower, three packets. Green Bean, two packets. Pumpkin, nothing at all. Nobody had signed out a single pumpkin seed. Yet for the third week in a row, every pumpkin packet had vanished.\n\n“Miss Ana,” Maya called. “Someone keeps taking the pumpkin seeds without writing it down.”\n\nMiss Ana peered into the drawer. “How strange,” she said. “Well, I suppose it isn't the end of the world. Seeds are meant to be shared.”\n\nBut Maya could not stop thinking about it. The seed library worked because people were honest. If someone took seeds without signing them out, how would anyone know to bring seeds back in the fall? Next year, the drawer might stay empty forever.\n\nAt lunch, she told her best friend, Luis Ortega, who lived across the street from the library. Luis loved mystery books, and his eyes lit up as she talked.\n\n“A real case,” he said. “We need to investigate.”\n\n“How?” Maya asked.\n\nLuis grinned. “First, we refill the drawer. Then we watch it very, very closely.”\n\nThat afternoon, Maya folded twelve new envelopes and filled each one with flat, cream-colored pumpkin seeds. She counted them twice. Then she slid them into the drawer and closed it.\n\n“Twelve packets,” she whispered to Luis. “Let's see how long they last.”","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"When do people bring seeds back to the seed library?","options":["in the fall","in the spring","every Saturday"],"answer":0,"evidence":"In the fall, they brought those seeds back so that someone else could plant them next year."},{"type":"why","q":"Why is Maya worried about the missing pumpkin seeds?","options":["Without records, nobody may return pumpkin seeds next year.","Miss Ana told her she would lose her volunteer job soon.","She wanted to plant all of the pumpkin seeds herself."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Luis's reaction show about him?","options":["He is excited to solve a real mystery.","He thinks Maya took the seeds herself.","He wants Miss Ana to close the library."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Maya is careful and organized?","options":["She counted them twice.","It was empty again.","Luis loved mystery books, and his eyes lit up as she talked."],"answer":0,"evidence":"She counted them twice."}],"grade":4,"words":382,"auto":{"fk":3.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-book-ch2","unit":"G4-U4","pool":"book","book":"The Seed Library Mystery","chapter":2,"title":"Chapter 2: The Note in the Drawer","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"For the next week, Luis checked the seed cabinet every day after school. On Monday, all twelve pumpkin packets were still there. On Tuesday and Wednesday, the drawer stayed full.\n\nOn Thursday, Luis ran into Maya's yard, out of breath. “Four packets are gone,” he panted. “And whoever took them left something behind.”\n\nThey hurried to the library together. Luis pulled open the Pumpkin drawer. Next to the eight remaining packets lay a small square of paper, folded in half. Maya unfolded it carefully.\n\nThe handwriting was large and shaky, as if it had been written slowly. It said: Thank you. I promise to pay you back in the fall.\n\nMaya read it twice. “A thief wouldn't say thank you,” she said slowly.\n\n“Maybe it's a trick,” said Luis, who had read a lot of mysteries.\n\nMaya looked at the floor in front of the cabinet. Near the bottom drawer were a few crumbs of dark, damp soil. She knelt down and touched one with her fingertip. It smelled like a garden after the rain.\n\n“Whoever it is,” she said, “they came here straight from digging.”\n\nThey asked Miss Ana if she had noticed anyone near the seed cabinet that afternoon. She thought for a moment. “It was a busy day,” she said. “But I did see an older gentleman in a green cap. He uses a cane, and he usually sits by the window with the newspaper.”\n\nOn Saturday, Maya and Luis sat at the table by the window and pretended to read. Maya's heart thumped every time the front door opened. At ten o'clock, a tall man in a faded green cap walked in. He leaned on a wooden cane. Under one arm he carried a canvas bag with a trowel poking out of the top.\n\nHe went straight to the seed cabinet, opened the Pumpkin drawer, and took two packets. Then he slipped a folded note into the drawer and walked back out into the sunshine.\n\nLuis jumped up. “Let's follow him!”\n\n“Not by ourselves,” Maya said firmly. “We don't know him.” She pointed through the window. The man was crossing the street toward Elm Street, where the town's community garden was. “But I know where he's going. We can ask my dad to take us there this afternoon.”\n\nLuis sighed, but he nodded. Even detectives, he admitted, needed a grown-up sometimes.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What did the man do right after he took two packets?","options":["He slipped a folded note into the drawer.","He sat down by the window with a newspaper.","He asked Miss Ana where the garden was."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Then he slipped a folded note into the drawer and walked back out into the sunshine."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports Maya's idea that the visitor had been digging?","options":["Near the bottom drawer were a few crumbs of dark, damp soil.","On Tuesday and Wednesday, the drawer stayed full.","Maya's heart thumped every time the front door opened."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Near the bottom drawer were a few crumbs of dark, damp soil."},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Maya mean when she says a thief wouldn't say thank you?","options":["She thinks the person means to return the seeds.","She thinks Luis wrote the note as a joke.","She thinks Miss Ana left the note for them."],"answer":0},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Maya refuse to follow the man right away?","options":["It is unsafe to follow a stranger alone.","She is afraid of losing her library job.","She wants to finish reading her book first."],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":396,"auto":{"fk":3.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-book-ch3","unit":"G4-U4","pool":"book","book":"The Seed Library Mystery","chapter":3,"title":"Chapter 3: Samuel's Garden","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The community garden on Elm Street was a patchwork of small plots, each one cared for by a different family. Maya's dad walked between the rows with Maya and Luis, saying hello to neighbors who were watering their beans and lettuce.\n\nAt the far end of the garden, they stopped and stared. One plot was covered in enormous green leaves, some as wide as umbrellas. Long vines crept over the edges and curled around the fence. Under the leaves, Maya could see dozens of small yellow flowers.\n\nIn the middle of it all, the man in the green cap was kneeling on a folded towel, pressing a seed into the soil with his thumb.\n\nMaya's dad cleared his throat. “Good afternoon,” he said. “These children are volunteers at the library, and they have a question about the pumpkin seeds.”\n\nThe man looked up. For a moment, his face turned pink. Then he slowly stood, leaning on his cane.\n\n“I suppose I owe you an explanation,” he said. “My name is Samuel.”\n\nSamuel told them that his wife, Ruth, had grown pumpkins in this same plot for thirty years. Every October, she gave them to the children at the family shelter on Pine Street so they could carve them for the fall festival. Ruth had passed away last winter. This spring, Samuel had decided to keep her tradition going.\n\n“But I'm not much of a gardener,” he said. “The first seeds I planted got washed away by the rain. The second batch, the squirrels dug up. So I kept coming back for more.”\n\n“Why didn't you sign the notebook?” Luis asked.\n\nSamuel sighed and tapped the thick glasses on his nose. “My eyes aren't good anymore. The lines in that notebook are so tiny, I couldn't read them. I was too embarrassed to ask for help. So I left notes instead.”\n\nMaya felt her cheeks grow warm. All week, she had been thinking of him as a thief. She had never once wondered if the person might need help.\n\n“We could have helped you,” she said quietly. “That's what the library is for.”\n\nSamuel smiled for the first time. “Then maybe you can help me now,” he said. “Do either of you know how to keep squirrels away from pumpkin seeds?”\n\nMaya and Luis looked at each other. Neither of them knew. But Maya's dad did. He grinned and rolled up his sleeves.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why does Samuel grow pumpkins in the community garden?","options":["To carry on his wife's yearly gift to children","To sell them at the farmers market on Pine Street","To win a prize for the biggest pumpkin in town"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best explains why Samuel never signed the notebook?","options":["The second batch, the squirrels dug up.","The lines in that notebook are so tiny, I couldn't read them.","Long vines crept over the edges and curled around the fence."],"answer":1,"evidence":"The lines in that notebook are so tiny, I couldn't read them."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Maya feel her cheeks grow warm?","options":["She feels bad for assuming Samuel was a thief.","She is hot from walking in the summer sun.","She is angry that Samuel took so many seeds."],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"In this chapter, what does the word tradition mean?","options":["something done the same way year after year","a garden plot shared by many families","a note left for someone to find later"],"answer":0,"evidence":"This spring, Samuel had decided to keep her tradition going."}],"grade":4,"words":402,"auto":{"fk":3.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g4-book-ch4","unit":"G4-U4","pool":"book","book":"The Seed Library Mystery","chapter":4,"title":"Chapter 4: Paying It Back","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"That summer, the pumpkin vines in Samuel's plot grew and grew. Maya's dad helped him cover the young seedlings with wire screens to keep the squirrels out. Luis came over twice a week to help water. Maya brought her notebook and kept a careful record of every new flower and every tiny green pumpkin.\n\nMeanwhile, Maya had an idea for the library. She and Luis made a brand new sign-out sheet with huge, bold letters and wide spaces for writing. They tied a magnifying glass to the cabinet with a ribbon. At the top of the sheet, Maya wrote: Need help? Just ask a volunteer.\n\nMiss Ana loved it. “I never thought about how hard the old notebook was to read,” she said. “You noticed something important.”\n\nIn late September, the pumpkins turned from green to deep orange. Samuel showed Maya how to save seeds from one of them. They scooped out the stringy insides, rinsed the seeds in a bowl of water, and spread them on paper towels to dry in the sun.\n\nOn the first Saturday in October, Samuel walked into the library carrying a big glass jar. It was filled to the top with flat, cream-colored seeds.\n\n“I promised to pay you back in the fall,” he said, setting it on the counter. “I believe I borrowed about twenty packets. This should fill about two hundred.”\n\nMaya laughed out loud. She and Luis spent the whole afternoon folding envelopes. When they were done, the Pumpkin drawer was so full they could barely close it.\n\nTwo weeks later, Maya, Luis, and Maya's dad helped Samuel load forty pumpkins into a borrowed truck. At the family shelter on Pine Street, children came running outside. Some of the pumpkins were small enough to hold in one hand. Others were so big that two kids had to carry them together. That evening, the shelter's yard glowed with carved faces lit by candles.\n\nSamuel stood beside Maya and watched. “Ruth would have loved this,” he said softly.\n\nOn Monday, Maya opened the green notebook to write down the new pumpkin seeds. Then she paused and turned to a fresh page. At the top, she wrote a new title: Things We Learned.\n\nUnder it, she wrote only one line: Before you decide what someone did wrong, ask what they might need.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"What is the theme of the whole novel?","options":["Ask questions and offer help before judging others.","Pumpkins grow best when they are kept from squirrels.","Libraries should only lend books instead of seeds."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What did Maya and Luis tie to the seed cabinet?","options":["a magnifying glass","a green notebook","a pair of glasses"],"answer":0,"evidence":"They tied a magnifying glass to the cabinet with a ribbon."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Samuel kept his promise?","options":["It was filled to the top with flat, cream-colored seeds.","Samuel stood beside Maya and watched.","Some of the pumpkins were small enough to hold in one hand."],"answer":0,"evidence":"It was filled to the top with flat, cream-colored seeds."},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of this chapter?","options":["Samuel returns many seeds, and his pumpkins bring joy to children at the shelter.","Maya and Luis plant forty pumpkins and carve them for the library festival.","Miss Ana finds out who took the seeds and buys a new notebook for the library."],"answer":0}],"grade":4,"words":390,"auto":{"fk":4.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}}];
